Professor Peter Postl is Director of Education and Professor (Teaching) in the Department of Economics at University College London (UCL). A UCL alumnus, he holds a PhD and MSc in Economics from UCL and returned to the department in 2022 after holding academic roles at the Universities of Bath and Birmingham.
&nbsp;
In his previous role as Head of Department of Economics at Bath, he led strategic reforms in curriculum design, employability, and research infrastructure. He developed interdisciplinary programmes, established an Employer Advisory Board, and oversaw significant growth in student numbers and research visibility.
&nbsp;
Professor Postl is committed to inclusive education and academic leadership. He played a key role in Bath’s Athena SWAN Bronze Award and serves on the Royal Economic Society’s Conference of Heads of University Departments of Economics (CHUDE)&nbsp;Steering Group. At UCL, he leads efforts to enhance teaching quality, curriculum innovation, and student experience, ensuring that economics education remains rigorous, inclusive, and future-focused.

tabSummaryTeachingActivities.htmlStripped:
Professor Peter Postl has extensive teaching expertise in microeconomics, game theory, strategic decision-making, and quantitative methods. Throughout his career, he has taught a wide variety of modules in economics, introducing students to fundamental principles as well as advanced topics such as game theory, mechanism design, regulation, and competition policy.
&nbsp;
As an educator, Professor Postl is committed to engaging, research-informed teaching and student success. He has received consistently high student satisfaction scores, was awarded a University of Bath Students’ Union Excellence in Teaching Award, and was nominated for a UCL Students' Union Student Choice Award for Inspiring Teaching Delivery. He has also led innovations in blended and online learning, including the design of a complete postgraduate online programme.

Dr. Tincani is an award-winning university instructor, having been awarded the 2019 Provost's Education Award (awarded to 10 individuals out of around 8,000 teaching staff) for outstanding contributions to education. She specialises in teaching complex material in a simple-to-understand and engaging way, and this is reflected in consistently good student evaluations.Recognising the importance of teaching material that is relevant in today's world, Dr. Tincani designed and introduced into the BSc in Economics programme a course on the Economics of Inequality.
&nbsp;
Dr. Tincani has taught PhD-level courses at UCL, Queen's University, and in the prestigious Econometric Society Summer School in Dynamic Structural Econometrics.
